3.1. Deploying an IoT System Using ESP32 and Linux IPC
3.1.1. Overview of the Deployment Process
The system is built on an IoT model, combining the ESP32-S embedded device with an Ubuntu Linux server to collect, transmit, and process sensor data. The ESP32-S reads temperature and humidity data from the DHT22 sensor, then packages the data as JSON and sends it to the server over Wi-Fi using an HTTPS connection. On the server, Flask receives the request and uses an inter-process communication (IPC) mechanism through a Queue to pass the data to the Worker process for processing.
After processing is complete, the Worker returns the result to Flask through an output Queue. Flask packages the result into a JSON response and sends it back to the ESP32-S. The device uses the received information to display the temperature status on a 16×2 LCD connected over I2C.
The deployment process is organized into the following main stages:
1.	Assemble the hardware and determine the connection pins.
2.	Set up the programming environment and build the ESP32 program in the Arduino IDE.
3.	Set up the Python and Flask environment on Ubuntu Linux.
4.	Build the Worker process and the IPC Queue mechanism.
5.	Test the Flask server in the local environment.
6.	Create a Cloudflare Quick Tunnel to connect the ESP32 to the server.
7.	Integrate the ESP32 with the server and test the entire data flow.
8.	Collect performance figures and evaluate the results.

